      {
        "review_id": 483027638,
        "review_link": "https://www.tripadvisor.com/ShowUserReviews-g293890-d310712-r483027638-Pashupatinath_Temple-Kathmandu_Kathmandu_Valley_Bagmati_Zone_Central_Region.html",
        "title": "Outstanding - must visit!",
        "text": "As someone interested in culture and religions this location was the highlight of my visit to Kathmandu. In typical Nepalese style it is confusing with no instructions for foreigners but find a little wooden hut and pay 1000NPR and get a guide. Our guide took our tickets after we had used them in order to get some sort of payment (maybe?) but we also tipped him. Our guide was extremely knowledgeable and took us everywhere (except the main temple which is for Hindus only). The highlight of the visit itself was witnessing the cremation of an ex-Gurkha. As the family attended to the cremation the last post was played by a group of Gurkhas. We saw very few foreigners on our tour of the site and felt immersed in the culture. Near the temple there were many Hindu pilgrims very happy to be there and taking lots of photos and this contrasted sharply to the scenes of cremation which were ritualistically formal and somber with family members fully involved with proceedings. Not at all like \"hands-off\" western cremations. The whole place was an assault on our senses. It was a hot day, there were fires for devotion, groups of Hindus in religious services, goats and cows walking about unhindered, lots of monkeys (don't touch!) and pigeons (also having a favoured status). There were strong smells, funeral processions, statues and shrines to different gods, fabulous architecture, sadhus (our guide called some of them fake and others genuine). Also many people just hanging about soaking up the atmosphere and having picnics. This place was authentic and was a good example of lived contemporary religion rather than a relic to the past.",
        "review_tip": null,
        "rating": 5,
